.page cmp 1 compare two files byte by byte

# SYNOPSIS
*cmp* [*-sl*] _FILE1_ _FILE2_

# DESCRIPTION
`cmp` compares two files. If they are identical it prints nothing. At the first difference it prints the byte number and line number, for example `a b differ: byte 9, line 2`.

# OPTIONS
@ *-s*
    Silent: print nothing, only set the exit status.
@ *-l*
    Print every differing byte: its position in decimal followed by the two byte values in octal. The comparison does not stop at the first difference.

If one file is a prefix of the other, `cmp: EOF on FILE after byte N` is printed on standard error.

# EXIT STATUS
0 if the files are the same, 1 if they differ, 2 if a file could not be opened or the usage was wrong.

# EXAMPLES
    cmp a.bin b.bin
    cmp -s a.txt b.txt && echo same

# SEE ALSO
comm(1), md5sum(1), sha256sum(1)
